<Docs
	page="recipes/swift"
	title="Swift"
	lede="GRDB, SQLite.swift, and PostgresNIO work out of the box."
>
	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			Multi-line string literals (<code>"""</code>) without interpolation.
			squill lays the query out between the quotes, with the closing{" "}
			<code>"""</code> at the statement&rsquo;s indent and every line of SQL at
			or past it, which is how Swift measures the string&rsquo;s indentation.
			Single-line strings are left exactly as written, and so is a string whose{" "}
			<code>\(&hellip;)</code> interpolation pastes text into the SQL. One the
			library sends as a parameter &mdash; GRDB&rsquo;s <code>literal:</code>,
			PostgresNIO&rsquo;s <code>query</code>, SQLKit&rsquo;s{" "}
			<code>\(bind: &hellip;)</code> &mdash; is formatted around, and written
			back as it was. Escapes come back exactly as written. Raw strings (
			<code>#"""</code>) aren&rsquo;t taken either.
		</p>
		<p>
			The SQL is indented the way the file is, so a project indented with four
			spaces gets four-space SQL.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>GRDB</h2>
		<p>
			The built-in <code>swift</code> grammar&rsquo;s default query takes any
			string passed as a <code>sql:</code> argument &mdash;{" "}
			<code>db.execute(sql:)</code>, <code>Row.fetchAll(db, sql:)</code>, and
			the rest of GRDB&rsquo;s API &mdash; and reads it as SQLite, whatever the
			configured dialect. No query file needed:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="swift-grdb" lang="swift" />
		<p>
			GRDB&rsquo;s <code>?</code> and <code>:name</code> arguments are
			SQLite&rsquo;s own, so they pass through as written.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>SQLite.swift</h2>
		<p>
			The default query also takes the first argument of <code>run</code>,{" "}
			<code>execute</code>, <code>prepare</code>, and <code>scalar</code>, in
			the configured dialect &mdash; so set the rule to SQLite:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="swift-sqlite" lang="swift" />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>PostgresNIO and SQLKit</h2>
		<p>
			PostgresNIO&rsquo;s <code>query</code> and SQLKit&rsquo;s <code>raw</code>{" "}
			take their SQL first, too. PostgresNIO binds every interpolation (
			<code>\(id)</code>) as a parameter, and SQLKit binds a labeled one (
			<code>\(bind: id)</code>), so squill formats around them and writes them
			back as they were:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="swift-postgresnio" lang="swift" />
	</section>
</Docs>
